\mt1 Psalms

\c 24

\d Of David. A psalm.

\s1 The Earth Is the LORD's

\b
\q1
\v 1 The earth is the LORD's —\f + \fr 24:1 \fk KONTEXT \fq The earth is the LORD's \ft This opening line is not devotional praise. It is a legal claim — a declaration of cosmic ownership that strips every empire, every corporation, every king of their pretension to absolute ownership. In the ancient world, this was polemic: against Marduk who "owned" Babylon, against Pharaoh who "owned" Egypt, against any local deity's territorial claim. The sentence says: if God owns everything, no human does absolutely. You are tenants, not owners. — This verse has been misused in settler-colonial theology to sanction land seizure — "God's people" as proxies for divine ownership. The psalm says the opposite. It denies any human absolute claim. The earth belongs to God, which means it belongs to no empire. — The psalm is the Sunday psalm of the Jewish Tamid service (Mishnah Tamid 7:4) — the week begins by acknowledging that the world is not ours. It is also chanted during the Torah return in synagogue: the Torah scroll is carried back to the Ark while vv.7-10 are sung, identifying the Torah itself as the King of Glory arriving. No single Christian reading captures the density of this psalm's living Jewish liturgical home.\f*
\q1 and everything in it.
\q1 The world and all who live here.

\b
\q1
\v 2 He planted it on the seas,\f + \fr 24:2 \fk KONTEXT \fq the seas... the currents \ft The "seas" and "currents" here are not geography. They are the primordial chaos forces from ancient Near Eastern cosmology — the same mythological pair (*yam* and *nahar*) that appears in the Ugaritic Baal Cycle, where the sea-god Yamm threatens cosmic order. The image is of God establishing the world by subduing these forces — like driving foundation piles into a swamp. The world holds because God planted it there, against the chaos that surrounds it.\f*
\q1 set it firm over the currents.

\s1 Who Can Stand Here

\b
\q1
\v 3 Who can go up the LORD's mountain?
\q1 Who can stand in his holy place?

\b
\q1
\v 4 Clean hands.\f + \fr 24:4 \fk KONTEXT \fq Clean hands... uncorrupted heart \ft These are ethical conditions, not ritual ones. The Hebrew words for "clean" and "pure" here do not belong to the vocabulary of ritual purification — they are integrity language. The four items form a chiasm: outer conduct (hands) / inner attitude (heart) / inner falsehood (not giving yourself to what is empty) / outer deceit (not swearing falsely). The question is not "have you performed the right rituals?" but "are your relationships honest — with God and with the people around you?" This is anti-corruption language. In a world of false oaths and bloodied hands, the psalm asks: who has kept their integrity intact?\f*
\q1 Uncorrupted heart.
\q1 Someone who does not give themselves to what is false,
\q1 who has not sworn a lying oath.

\b
\q1
\v 5 They will carry away a blessing from the LORD,\f + \fr 24:5 \fk TEXT \fq carry away a blessing... vindication \ft The Hebrew word *vindication* here (*tsedaqah*) does not primarily mean moral quality or personal righteousness. It means relational vindication — God declaring in favor of the person whose integrity is intact. It is the saving fidelity of a God who backs up those who kept their hands clean when everyone around them didn't. — The verb "carry away" is the same Hebrew root (*nasa*) as "does not give themselves" in v.4 and "Lift your heads" in vv.7 and 9. This is the poet's structural design: the one who refuses to carry falsehood is the one who carries away blessing; the gates that lift up perform the cosmic version of the same verb. One root word threads the entire psalm.\f*
\q1 vindication from the God who saves them.

\b
\q1
\v 6 This is the company of those who seek him —\f + \fr 24:6 \fk KONTEXT \fq the company... Jacob \ft "Jacob" at the end of this verse identifies the community of seekers as wrestlers — those who struggle with God (Genesis 32). The word *dor* ("company" or "generation") is not just an age-group but a relational community: the people defined by the act of seeking rather than by lineage or ritual status. Scholars identify this verse as a post-exilic addition that democratizes the entrance question — the true community is not the priestly elite but the poor who seek because they have nowhere else to turn.\f*
\q1 those who search for your face, Jacob.

\s1 The King of Glory

\b
\q1
\v 7 Lift your heads, you gates.\f + \fr 24:7 \fk KONTEXT \fq Lift your heads, you gates... ancient doors \ft The gates are personified — cosmic infrastructure being commanded to acknowledge sovereignty. The image is borrowed from the Ugaritic Baal Cycle, where gods in the divine council lift their heads in acknowledgment when a victorious king arrives. The command is the entire universe being ordered to recognize who its ruler is. — "Ancient doors" translates Hebrew *pitchei olam* — literally "doors of *olam*." The word *olam* means both "age-old" (ancient in time) and "eternal" (permanent in quality). Both senses are intentional. These are doors that have stood since the beginning and will stand forever. — "The King of Glory" (*melek hakavod*) is a title that appears nowhere else in the entire Hebrew Bible — a hapax, a unique throne name. The root of *kavod* (glory) is *kaved* — heaviness, weight, substance. The King of Glory is the King whose weight nothing can resist. — In Jewish liturgical practice, these verses are chanted as the Torah scroll is returned to the Ark. The Ark doors open; the congregation sings "Lift your heads, you gates"; the Torah enters as the King of Glory. This practice continues unbroken in synagogues worldwide.\f*
\q1 Rise up, you ancient doors.
\q1 The King of Glory is coming in.

\b
\q1
\v 8 Who is this King of Glory?\f + \fr 24:8 \fk TEXT \fq the warrior of battles \ft The Hebrew *gibbor milchamah* means a physical combatant — a battlefield warrior, not a metaphorical "mighty one." The psalm identifies God specifically as someone who fights. David Clines argued that this language writes "a blank check for war." John Goldingay countered: "It would be at least as plausible to suggest that we are making war God's business rather than ours and writing a blank check for pacifism." The tension is real and unresolved. The psalm does not tell you which reading is correct.\f*
\q1
\q1 The LORD — fierce and mighty,
\q1 the LORD — the warrior of battles.

\b
\q1
\v 9 Lift your heads, you gates.
\q1 Rise high, you ancient doors.
\q1 The King of Glory is coming in.

\b
\q1
\v 10 Who is he — this King of Glory?\f + \fr 24:10 \fk TEXT \fq Who is he... the LORD of armies \ft The second round of the gate drama is not redundant — it is more insistent. Three escalations: (1) "Who is he" adds an emphatic pronoun absent from v.8's question; (2) "the LORD of armies" (*YHWH tsevaot*) surpasses v.8's "warrior of battles" — this is not one fighter but the commander of the entire heavenly host; (3) the final "He is the King of Glory" adds the emphatic pronoun again — a clinching identification. The universe has its answer. The title *YHWH tsevaot* is a throne name — the name God bears as the royal resident of Zion, the one whose power makes the city of God invulnerable.\f*
\q1
\q1 The LORD of armies —
\q1 he is the King of Glory.
